{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":214932,"duration_api_ms":213781,"num_turns":8,"result":"","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"5665d0d9-6d01-42d1-aaf1-781cbaa3710c","total_cost_usd":0.7128963500000001,"usage":{"input_tokens":7,"cache_creation_input_tokens":42424,"cache_read_input_tokens":225294,"output_tokens":8830,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":42424,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":7,"outputTokens":8830,"cacheReadInputTokens":225294,"cacheCreationInputTokens":42424,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.598582,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000},"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":57,"outputTokens":3792,"cacheReadInputTokens":268911,"cacheCreationInputTokens":54725,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.11431434999999998,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000}},"permission_denials":[],"structured_output":{"summary":"Reviewed both amendments against the codebase. Amendment fr-6-2ac10d07 is rejected: the migration preserves concentrations by construction (rescaling totals by net/gross cancels when dividing by net_volume), so prev_gh_d/prev_do_mg_l remain valid. Amendment fr-6-1af96a54 is accepted: the stale water-column snapshot causes the second guild's health_index assessment to be incorrectly optimistic since nutrient_limitation is computed from pre-depletion concentrations while the actual pools have been depleted by earlier guilds.","votes":[{"amendment_id":"fr-6-2ac10d07","decision":"reject","rationale":"The amendment's claim of a false instability spike is mathematically incorrect. In schema v2, concentrations used gross volume semantics (the comment at save.rs:11-13 explicitly states this). The stored prev_gh_d was computed as f(total_old / gross_volume). After migrate_schema_v2_to_v3, rescale_totals_for_volume(gross, net) scales every total by net/gross. The new gh_d computed via state.concentrations() (which uses net_volume) gives: (2.497 * calcium_total_old * (net/gross) / net_volume + ...) / K = (2.497 * calcium_total_old / gross_volume + ...) / K = prev_gh_d. The same identity holds for do_mg_per_l since it is also total/volume. The concentrations are preserved by construction — the rescaling factor in the totals cancels exactly with the volume change in the denominator. Therefore prev_gh_d and prev_do_mg_l remain valid baselines, and update_stability_tracker will compute zero swing on the first post-load tick. No reseed is needed for v2 saves that already have a stability_tracker."},{"amendment_id":"fr-6-1af96a54","decision":"accept","rationale":"The bug is real and verified in code. At plant_growth.rs:9-12, tan_mg_n_per_l, nitrate_mg_n_per_l, and phosphate_mg_p_per_l are captured once before the guild loop. Inside the loop, remove_plant_nutrients() (line 93-94) mutates state.water.ammonia_total_mg_n_total, nitrate_mg_n_total, and phosphate_mg_p_total. The second guild's water_n_factor and water_p_factor (lines 59-64) are computed from the stale pre-depletion snapshot, not the current depleted pools. While the actual biomass growth is safely capped by cap_frac (lines 98-112 ensure growth doesn't exceed available nutrients), the health_index update at line 142 uses the uncapped nutrient_limitation directly: stress_driver = nutrient_limitation.min(f_light).min(habitat_index). The second guild's health assessment sees abundant nutrients that were already consumed, preventing appropriate health decline under competitive conditions. Over many daily steps this compounds — later guilds maintain artificially high health, reducing their senescence and giving them an unfair competitive advantage. The fix is straightforward: recompute water-column concentrations inside the loop after each guild's nutrient removal."}]},"fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"fc6c07d2-bdac-4b4e-993a-8247a611c823"}
